{
  "id": "COMPOUND:537 - PROTEIN:685",
  "verbalization": "Acetohydroxamic acid appears to interact with Serine/threonine-protein phosphatase 2A catalytic subunit beta isoform through a multi-step pathway, where it acts as a target of multiple compounds. The evidence suggests that acetohydroxamic acid is a target of 72 kDa type IV collagenase, which in turn is targeted by N-[(3-hydroxy-4-methoxyphenyl)methyl]-7-methyloctanamide and Spongia-13(16),14-Dien-19-Oic Acid. These compounds are all linked to the Serine/threonine-protein phosphatase 2A catalytic subunit beta isoform, indicating a potential role for these molecules in modulating the activity of this phosphatase. The shared target relationship implies that acetohydroxamic acid may be involved in a broader regulatory network, possibly through the activation or inhibition of this phosphatase. The integration of these pathways highlights the complex interplay between acetohydroxamic acid and the phosphatase, suggesting a possible mechanism involving the regulation of extracellular matrix components or cellular signaling pathways.",
